<codeline><highlight class="normal">#<sp/>FFT<sp/>vs.<sp/>GPU<sp/>Convolution<sp/>and<sp/>Morphological<sp/>Expansion</highlight></codeline>
<codeline></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal">This<sp/>document<sp/>records<sp/>the<sp/>experimental<sp/>evaluation<sp/>of<sp/>2D<sp/>Fast<sp/>Fourier<sp/>Transform<sp/>(FFT)<sp/>algorithms<sp/>as<sp/>potential<sp/>alternatives<sp/>to<sp/>spatial<sp/>GPU<sp/>implementations<sp/>for<sp/>convolution<sp/>and<sp/>morphological<sp/>operations.</highlight></codeline>
<codeline></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal">Related<sp/>issue:<sp/>[#528](https://github.com/ottolink-dev/HighMap/issues/528).</highlight></codeline>
<codeline></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal">##<sp/>1.<sp/>Context<sp/>and<sp/>Motivation</highlight></codeline>
<codeline></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal">Spatial<sp/>filtering<sp/>on<sp/>large<sp/>kernels<sp/>can<sp/>become<sp/>computationally<sp/>demanding<sp/>with<sp/>direct<sp/>convolution,<sp/>which<sp/>scales<sp/>with<sp/>the<sp/>kernel<sp/>area<sp/>$\mathcal{O}(K^2)$.<sp/>In<sp/>contrast,<sp/>frequency-domain<sp/>convolution<sp/>via<sp/>FFT<sp/>operates<sp/>in<sp/>$\mathcal{O}(N<sp/>\log<sp/>N)$<sp/>independent<sp/>of<sp/>kernel<sp/>radius:</highlight></codeline>
<codeline></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal">$$f<sp/>*<sp/>g<sp/>=<sp/>\mathcal{F}^{-1}\{\mathcal{F}\{f\}<sp/>\cdot<sp/>\mathcal{F}\{g\}\}$$</highlight></codeline>
<codeline></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal">Similarly,<sp/>morphological<sp/>expansion<sp/>(dilation<sp/>/<sp/>maximum<sp/>filter)<sp/>can<sp/>theoretically<sp/>be<sp/>approximated<sp/>in<sp/>the<sp/>Fourier<sp/>domain<sp/>via<sp/>a<sp/>soft-max<sp/>formulation:</highlight></codeline>
<codeline></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal">$$\max_i<sp/>x_i<sp/>\approx<sp/>\frac{1}{\beta}<sp/>\ln<sp/>\sum_i<sp/>e^{\beta<sp/>x_i}$$</highlight></codeline>
<codeline></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal">which<sp/>can<sp/>be<sp/>evaluated<sp/>using<sp/>FFT<sp/>convolutions<sp/>of<sp/>exponentiated<sp/>fields.</highlight></codeline>
<codeline></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal">Two<sp/>experiments<sp/>were<sp/>conducted<sp/>to<sp/>evaluate<sp/>whether<sp/>FFT-based<sp/>methods<sp/>provide<sp/>performance<sp/>or<sp/>scaling<sp/>advantages<sp/>over<sp/>existing<sp/>GPU<sp/>implementations:</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal">1.<sp/>FFT<sp/>2D<sp/>convolution<sp/>(`convolve2d_fft`)<sp/>vs.<sp/>GPU<sp/>separable<sp/>convolution<sp/>(`smooth_cpulse`).</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal">2.<sp/>FFT<sp/>soft-max<sp/>morphological<sp/>expansion<sp/>(`expand_soft_fft`)<sp/>vs.<sp/>GPU<sp/>morphological<sp/>expansion<sp/>(`expand`).</highlight></codeline>
<codeline></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal">##<sp/>2.<sp/>Experiment<sp/>1:<sp/>2D<sp/>Convolution</highlight></codeline>
<codeline></codeline>
