<codeline></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal">###<sp/>Findings</highlight></codeline>
<codeline></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal">The<sp/>FFT<sp/>implementation<sp/>is<sp/>consistently<sp/>slower<sp/>across<sp/>all<sp/>tested<sp/>configurations,<sp/>with<sp/>a<sp/>performance<sp/>penalty<sp/>ranging<sp/>from<sp/>$3\times$<sp/>to<sp/>$22\times$.</highlight></codeline>
<codeline></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal">While<sp/>the<sp/>performance<sp/>gap<sp/>narrows<sp/>as<sp/>the<sp/>mesh<sp/>size<sp/>increases,<sp/>the<sp/>GPU<sp/>separable<sp/>convolution<sp/>remains<sp/>significantly<sp/>faster<sp/>even<sp/>at<sp/>$512\times512$.<sp/>For<sp/>separable<sp/>filters<sp/>(such<sp/>as<sp/>standard<sp/>smooth<sp/>pulses<sp/>and<sp/>Gaussian-like<sp/>kernels),<sp/>the<sp/>$\mathcal{O}(2K)$<sp/>complexity<sp/>of<sp/>separable<sp/>passes<sp/>combined<sp/>with<sp/>GPU<sp/>memory<sp/>bandwidth<sp/>efficiency<sp/>outperforms<sp/>the<sp/>forward<sp/>and<sp/>inverse<sp/>FFT<sp/>transform<sp/>pipeline.</highlight></codeline>
<codeline></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal">Conclusion:<sp/>FFT-based<sp/>convolution<sp/>does<sp/>not<sp/>provide<sp/>a<sp/>performance<sp/>advantage<sp/>over<sp/>the<sp/>existing<sp/>GPU<sp/>separable<sp/>convolution.</highlight></codeline>
<codeline></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal">##<sp/>3.<sp/>Experiment<sp/>2:<sp/>Morphological<sp/>Expansion</highlight></codeline>
<codeline></codeline>
